Which of the following statements are true?
$$\begin{array}{ll}
        \text{I.} & \text{The equation } x^3 + y^3 = 2029 \text{ has no integer solutions.} \\
        \text{II.} & \text{The equation } x^2 + y^2 = 2027 \text{ has no integer solutions.} \\
        \text{III.} & \text{The equation } x^3 + y^3 = 1027 \text{ has no integer solutions.}
        \end{array}$$ Hint: consider remainders.

Let $I$ be an interval of real numbers, and let $f$ be a continuous function defined on $I$ such that $f(x) \in I$ for all $x \in I$ (we say $f$ maps $I$ into itself). A number $c \in I$ is called a fixed point of $f$ if $f(c) = c$.

Consider the following statements:

$
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{I.} & \text{If } I = [0,1] \text{, then } f \text{ has at least one fixed point.} \\
\text{II.} & \text{If } I = (0,1) \text{, then } f \text{ has at least one fixed point.} \\
\text{III.} & \text{If } I = [0,1] \text{ and } f \text{ is strictly decreasing, then } f \text{ has exactly one fixed point.}
\end{array}
$
Which of these must be true?

A binary string is a sequence of digits, each $0$ or $1$. A run in a binary string is a maximal block of consecutive equal digits; for example, the string $0011101$ has four runs. Consider the following statements about binary strings of length $10$:

$
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{I.} & \text{Exactly } 2 \text{ such strings have exactly one run.} \\
\text{II.} & \text{The number of such strings with exactly } 4 \text{ runs equals the number with exactly } 7 \text{ runs.} \\
\text{III.} & \text{Strictly more such strings have exactly } 5 \text{ runs than have any other number of runs.}
\end{array}
$
Which of these statements are true?

For a function $f$ defined on the positive integers and a positive integer $a_1$, define a sequence by $a_{k+1} = f(a_k)$. Say that $f$ terminates if, for every choice of $a_1$, the sequence eventually takes the value $1$.

Consider the following statements:

$
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{I.} & \text{If } f(n) = \frac{n}{2} \text{ for even } n \text{ and } f(n) = n+1 \text{ for odd } n \text{, then } f \text{ terminates.} \\
\text{II.} & \text{If } f(n) = \frac{n}{2} \text{ for even } n \text{ and } f(n) = 3n-1 \text{ for odd } n \text{, then } f \text{ terminates.} \\
\text{III.} & \text{If } f(n) = \frac{n}{2} \text{ for even } n \text{ and } f(n) = 3n+1 \text{ for odd } n \text{, then for every choice of } a_1 \text{, no two consecutive terms of the sequence are both odd.}
\end{array}
$
Which of these statements are true?
